WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.300
Effective threat hunting depends on meaningful telemetry.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.950
Analysts need data that reflects activity across important systems and enough context to

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.680
distinguish expected operations from behavior that deserves further review.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.740
Endpoint telemetry can describe system activity, software execution, security events,

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.600
connections, and changes over time.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.995
Its value increases when records include stable asset identifiers, accurate timestamps,

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:24.625
ownership, and business context.

00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:27.936
Authentication and identity records help hunters understand access patterns.

00:00:27.936 --> 00:00:30.950
Sign-in events, privilege changes, account lifecycle information,

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.168
and identity-provider context can show whether activity aligns with a user's role and

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.960
normal operating conditions.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.335
Network telemetry provides another perspective.

00:00:39.335 --> 00:00:42.755
Connection metadata, name-resolution records, approved security-device logs,

00:00:42.755 --> 00:00:46.760
and traffic summaries can reveal relationships among systems without relying on a single

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.445
endpoint data source.

00:00:48.445 --> 00:00:50.840
Cloud and application logs are increasingly important.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.900
Control-plane events, workload records, application audit trails,

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.715
and service activity can help explain what occurred in environments that traditional

00:00:57.715 --> 00:01:00.165
network monitoring may not fully represent.

00:01:00.165 --> 00:01:03.410
Asset inventories and configuration information supply context.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.175
A record becomes more useful when the hunter knows whether the system is production or

00:01:07.175 --> 00:01:07.650
development,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:12.440
internet facing or internal, business critical or temporary, and managed by which team.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.950
Data quality determines investigative quality.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.840
Missing time periods, inconsistent clocks, duplicate records, short retention,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.221
unclear field definitions, and incomplete coverage can lead to incorrect conclusions.

00:01:23.221 --> 00:01:26.120
Hunts should document these limitations explicitly.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.255
Telemetry access also requires governance.

00:01:28.255 --> 00:01:31.060
Organizations should apply authorization, privacy, retention,

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.545
and need-to-know requirements so investigations use security data responsibly and maintain

00:01:35.545 --> 00:01:36.800
appropriate oversight.
